<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasion of Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1236">1236</a>–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mongol invasions of Europe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was the campaign of 1236–1242 in which Mongol armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and the principalities of Rus' and then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. They won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> and Mohi in April 1241, but withdrew in 1242, probably after learning of the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe.jpg" alt="Mongol invasion of Europe" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1236-1242_Mongol_invasions_of_Europe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
